.nh
.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-account - Show or update the signed-in account's profile (shows when run bare)


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 account [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Read and update the profile of the signed-in account: its id, name, email,
priority-tip setting and timestamps.

.PP
Requires a login ('rail0 auth login') by a wallet that belongs to an account — a
wallet-only session has no account to show. The account is the session's own
('rail0 auth status' prints its id); --account reads or updates another one, which
the gateway allows only for an admin (anyone else gets 403 not_your_account).

.PP
Run bare, or as 'account get', to show it; 'account update' changes the name,
email and/or priority tip. The account's active flag is operator-only and is not
settable here.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--account\fP=""
	Account id (default: the logged-in session account; another account's needs an admin session)

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for account


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--agent\fP[=false]
	Agent mode: one JSON envelope on stdout, JSON events on stderr, no prompts (env: RAIL0_AGENT=1)

.PP
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 account                          # the signed-in account
  rail0 account get --account 019e748b-… # another account (admin only)
  rail0 account update --email ops@example.com
  rail0 account update --priority-tip=false
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0(1)\fP, \fBrail0-account-get(1)\fP, \fBrail0-account-update(1)\fP
